#!/usr/bin/python3
"""Every hdw4s desktop in a network namespace of its own. Root only.

    hdw4s-netns up|pasta|ready|down <instance>   the net unit's four steps
    hdw4s-netns reload [<instance>...]           re-apply policy to running desktops
    hdw4s-netns backstop                         rebuild the container-side table only
    hdw4s-netns check                            report; exit 1 if anything is wrong
    hdw4s-netns lan list [<instance>]
    hdw4s-netns lan grant <instance> <host[:port]>...
    hdw4s-netns lan revoke <instance> <host[:port]>... | --all
    hdw4s-netns policy <instance>                what one desktop may reach, resolved
    hdw4s-netns parse <entry>...                 grammar only; exit 2 on a bad one
    hdw4s-netns print <instance> | print-backstop   the rulesets, unloaded

hdw4s(8), "NETWORK". The unit is hdw4s-net@.service; the desktop units Require
it, are ordered After it, and join the namespace with NetworkNamespacePath=.

WHAT A DESKTOP GETS. Its own loopback (an application reaching its own forked
copies works), and a way out through pasta(1), which turns the desktop's packets
into ordinary sockets of its own on the container's side. Nothing listening on
the container -- its loopback, its addresses, other desktops -- is reachable:
the namespace has its own copies of all of them. Out of the namespace, the
default is the public internet and nothing else: private, carrier-grade,
link-local and multicast IPv4, everything IPv6 outside 2000::/3, and the
container's own addresses are refused. On top of that, per desktop:

  * HDW4S_NET_ALLOW, essentials, layered machine -> pool -> instance like every
    other setting. An entry on the loopback (127.0.0.1:25) is a CONTAINER-LOCAL
    service and is carried by pasta's -T/-U port forward; anything else is a
    destination let through both tables.
  * the container's private upstream DNS resolvers, added without being asked:
    the namespace cannot reach a loopback stub, and its resolv.conf names the
    upstream ones instead.
  * grants (/etc/hdw4s/lan/<instance>), for a NAMED desktop only. Slot uids are
    reused by the next visitor, so no grant can ever reach a pooled slot.
  * HDW4S_NET_LAN=open lifts the private-range refusal (never the container's
    own addresses); "refused" is the default, for the pool too.

INTO the desktop, one thing only: where it offers WebRTC (HDW4S_WEBRTC=yes, the
default), ONE UDP port, forwarded by pasta from the container to this
namespace, which its streaming server binds every WebRTC candidate to; its
answers leave from that port only as replies. "WebRTC: one UDP port per
desktop" below says why that shape and which port.

TWO TABLES, ONE POLICY. Both are generated here from policy(), so they cannot
disagree about what a desktop may reach:

  * inet hdw4s_ns, INSIDE the namespace. This is what makes a refusal
    IMMEDIATE. Measured (2026-11-18): every verdict on the container's side
    hangs the application for its full timeout, because this pasta does not
    turn a host-side refusal into one the application can see; a tcp reset or
    an ICMP error made inside the namespace arrives in milliseconds. It is
    loaded BEFORE pasta attaches, so there is no instant with a way out and no
    table. It accepts nothing as "established": every outgoing packet is
    judged, so a revoked grant ends a live connection too.
  * inet hdw4s_net, in the CONTAINER: the backstop, keyed on the cgroup of the
    pasta units. Anything in hdw4s-net.slice is refused the private ranges and
    the container's own addresses; each RUNNING net unit is let through to what
    its own policy allows. nft resolves a cgroup path to a number when a rule is
    LOADED, so a restarted unit's old rules match nothing and the slice-wide
    refusal stands -- a stale grant fails closed. The table is rebuilt whole on
    every change, under a lock, from the units whose cgroups exist at that
    instant. A table of its own rather than chains in inet hdw4s, so that a
    rebuild at every desktop start never resets the cross-reach counters
    "hdw4s firewall --check" reads.

WHY PASTA RUNS AS ROOT, and the rules key on its cgroup rather than its uid:
--runas drops privileges BEFORE pasta joins the namespace and gets EPERM there
(measured, 2026-11-17, in an unprivileged container).

THE NAMESPACE OUTLIVES PASTA, NOT THE DESKTOP. It is made by "up" and removed by
"down" only when the desktop is not running. A pasta that dies is started again
by "pasta" itself, which supervises it, into the SAME namespace -- not by
systemd, whose restart of this unit would be carried to the desktop -- so the
desktop is neither stranded nor killed; while pasta is gone the desktop has
only lo, which is the closed direction. A pooled slot's next occupant always gets a new namespace, because
its desktop is stopped before its net unit is.

THE ANCHOR. hdw4s-demux decides whether a slot's desktop still answers by
looking for its stream socket in the kernel's table of listening unix sockets,
and that table is per network namespace: from the container, a desktop's
listener is simply not there. The router has no capabilities and cannot enter
a namespace, and a file the desktop writes could be forged by its occupant. So
"pasta" forks one root process into the namespace that does nothing but exist,
and bind-mounts its /proc/<pid>/net, read-only, at /run/hdw4s/net/<instance>/net.
Measured (2026-11-18): a DynamicUser reader with ProtectProc=invisible and no
capabilities, already running when the bind was made, reads the namespace's
own table there; the occupant cannot write through it; and once the anchor has
gone a read FAILS rather than returning an empty table. The anchor lives in
the net unit's cgroup and dies with it.

FAILS CLOSED. No /dev/net/tun, no pasta, a configuration that does not parse,
a table that does not load, a link that never comes up: the net unit fails, and
the desktop does not start (its NetworkNamespacePath= finds no namespace).
Each says what to do.
"""

